      "description": "Imbalance Analysis for Diagram 46.",
      "imageFilename": "diag_46_below.png",
      "textContext": "Diagram 46 \n[Level: 1400 - 1800] \nM. Tai - V. Soloviev, Riga 1955 \nWhite to move \n1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 Bg7 4.e4 0-0 5.Be3 d6 6.f3 e5 7.Nge2 c6 8.Qb3 Nbd7 \n9.0-0-0 Qe7 10.Kbl Re8 1 1.g4 a6 12.Ng3 Nf8 - Diagram 46 \nBlack just moved his Knight to f8 and clearly intends to play 1 3  . . .  N e6 when it will \nbe able to leap into d4 or f4. \nĮį.E.!œ.!3!3g--İıĲ-ĳ!_iŔ_g_ĴI!_!\"ĵĶ-ķ: The right move can be arrived at in \ndifferent ways. For example, if you simply wanted to gain space, then \nd5 is it. Or, if you were worried about . . .  exd4, then once again d5 is the \nmove. However, I am hoping that you noticed . . .  Ne6 and played d5 in a \ndeliberate attempt to stuff the enemy Knight! \nWhite now played 13.d5 because he intended to begin an attack by h2-h4-h5 and \nthe center needs to be closed to make this worthwhile-he also wanted to stop the f8-\nKnight from finding fame and fortune on e6. In other words, he used the advance of \nhis cl-pawn to dominate the enemy Knight and force it to remain on a purely defensive \nsquare. After 13 ... N6d7 14.h4 White had a clear advantage and went on to win. \n\n442 H O W  T O  R E A S S E S S  Y O U R  C H E S S  - 4 T H  E D I T I O N  \n.ANSWE.R \nP'AR.'.t two \n. T.EST 9",
