      "description": "Imbalance Analysis for Diagram 47.",
      "imageFilename": "diag_47_below.png",
      "textContext": "Diagram 47 \n[Level: 1 800 - 2000) \nR. Fischer - I. lbrahimoglu, Siegen 1970 \nWhite To Move \n1.e4 c6 2.d3 dS 3.Nd2 g6 4.Ngf3 Bg7 S.g3 Nf6 6.Bg2 0-0 7.0-0 Bg4 8.h3 \nBxfl 9.Qxf3 Nbd7 10.Qe2 dxe4 1 1.dxe4 Qc7 12.a4 Rad8 13.Nb3 b6 14.Be3 cS \n15.aS eS 16.Nd2 Ne8 17.axh6 axb6 - Diagram 47 \nThis is very nice for \\\\'bite: he has the two Bishops, the b5 and c4 squares can easily \nfall into his hands, and d5 is a gaping hole (compare this to d4, a square that is not a \nhole since it can be healed by the simple c2-c3). \nExplaining the Rating Spread: Yes, another Knight retreat! I would \nhave made the rating spread lower (since I know you guys are getting used \nto your horses going backwards), but the fact that the Knight is already \neyeing the nice c4-square, and that moves like Qb5 are also very tempting, \nmakes the problem rather difficult. \n18.Nbl! \nYou should be getting used to this idea by now! The Knight heads for c3 where it \nwill eye both b5 and d5. \n18 ... Qb7 \nOn 1 8  . . .  Qc6 19.Nd Nc7 20.Ra7 Ra8 \\\\'bite has 2 1 .Nd5 ! Nxd5 22.exd5 Qd6 \n23 .Rfa l Rxa7 24.Rxa7 Nf6 25.Qb5! and now 25 . . .  Nxd5 26.Rd7 Nc7 27.Rxd6 Nxb5 \n28.Rxb6 Nd4 29.d gives \\\\'bite a winning endgame thanks to the more active Rook, \nweakness of c5, and the power of white's two raking Bishops. \n19.Nc3 and white's position is clearly superior. The rest of the game was a beautiful \ndemonstration of technique by Fischer: \n19 ... Nc7 20.NbS \n20.Rfdl was also strong (and certainly the most natural looking move), but \\\\'bite \ndecides to swap the Knights and gain entry to b5 for his Queen. \n20 ... Qc6 21.Nxc7 Qxc7 22.QbS \nBlack's position is cheerless (two Bishops, pressure down the a-file, pressure against \nb6, more active pieces). Nobody could resist Fischer in positions of this nature. \n22 ... Ra8 23.c3 Rxal 24.Rxal Rb8 25.Ra6 \nThings are getting worse for Black with every move. \n\nA N S W E R S  T O  T E S T S  443 \n25 ... BfB 26.Bfl \nThe entry of this Bishop into the battle marks the beginning of the end. Notice \nhow Fischer makes sure every piece is in play. \n26 ... Kg7 27.Qa4 Rb7 28.Bb5 \nTotal domination-every white piece is superior to their black counterparts. \n28 ... NbS 29.RaS Bd6 30.Qdl Nc6 3 1.Qd2 \nI'll let the reader decide for himself whether or not the shocking 3 l .Bh6+ Kxh6 \n32.Rg8 does or doesn't work. \n3 1 .  .. h5 32.Bh6+ Kh7 33.Bg5 Rb8 34.RxbS Nxb8 35.Bf6 Nc6 \nAfter 3 5 . . .  Be7 36.Qg5 Bxf6 3 7 .Qxf6 we get a picturesque case of a Bishop dominat­\ning a Knight (white's Queen is also dominating the black Queen). \n36.Qd5 Na7 37.BeS Kg8 38.Bxf7+ Qxf7 39.Qxd6, 1 -0. \nPart Two I Minor Pieces - Bishops",
