      "imageFilename": "diag_72_below.png",
      "textContext": "Diagram 72\n[Level: 2000 on up]\nL. Aronian - P. Svidler, Morelia/Linares 2006\nWhite to move\n1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 d5 4.Bg5 Ne4 5.Bh4 c5 6.cxd5 Nxc3 7.bxc3 Qxd5 8.e3 cxd4 9.Qxd4 Qxd4 10.cxd4 e6 11.Rb1 Be7 12.Bxe7 Kxe7 13.Bd3 Nc6 14.Nf3 b6 15.Ke2 Bb7 16.Rhc1 Rhc8 - Diagram 72\nThe position is relatively open, but White has a slight space advantage and better piece coordination. Black's Bishop on b7 is currently blocked by the pawn on e6, but it can become active if ...e6-e5 is played. White's plan is to restrict Black's pieces and slowly build up his position. A key part of this is to control the c-file and prevent Black's Knight from finding a good square.\nExplaining the Rating Spread: This is a very deep positional battle. White's first move, g4, is designed to gain space on the kingside and prepare a kingside expansion. It's a move that requires a deep understanding of the position.\n17.g4!\nGaining space on the kingside and preparing a later h4-g5 expansion.\n17...h6 18.h4 Na5 19.g5 hxg5 20.hxg5 Rxc1 21.Rxc1 Rc8 22.Rxc8 Bxc8 23.Ne5 Bb7 24.a3 Nc6 25.Ng4 Nb8 26.Kd2 Bc6 27.Kc3 Nd7 28.f4 a5 29.e4 b5 30.Bc2 f5 31.exf5 exf5 32.Ne5 Nxe5 33.dxe5 b4+ 34.axb4 axb4+ 35.Kxb4 Bd7 36.Bb3 Bc6 37.Kc5 Be8 38.e6, 1-0.",
